cing near 97% all favor him, so the forecast sits on a large Democratic margin with the residual uncertainty mostly about the size of the win, not the winner.", "watch": "Fresh polling or a late wave of Republican outside spending could narrow the margin, though flipping the outcome would require an extraordinary shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; fundamentals point clearly one way (post-redistricting), so it is treated as safe for that party."]}, "overview": "Utah's new 1st District, created in redistricting, leans Democratic by about 12 points, and the open-seat contest features Ben McAdams, a former congressman and Salt Lake County mayor, against Republican Riley Owen. McAdams has raised roughly $2.7 million and drawn about $2.7 million in outside support, while Owen has raised under $150,000. A September poll of likely voters showed McAdams ahead 63 to 21, and prediction markets put his chances near 97 percent.\n\nDebates in late September and October focused on prices, artificial intelligence, and data centers, with the candidates often finding common ground, and no outside group has spent against McAdams. The district's fundamentals, the money gap, and market pricing all point the same way, making McAdams a strong favorite; the main open question is the size of his winning margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9908, "margin": 20.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.99, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9909, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9899, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9886, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9907, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.99, "margi